Dumps taken from a real riched20 on Windows 2000, one per sequence in the
directory above, with tools/vm/seqprobe.exe.

These are the other half of the differential test: `tests/replay_test` runs
the same sequence against ween32 and the two dumps are diffed. Committing
them means the comparison does not need a machine every time -- only when a
sequence is added or the contract changes.

Taken with replay.h and dump.h at ween32 db32b52: control 0x550081C4, ex
0x210, 280x160, SCF_DEFAULT Arial 10 with CFM_BOLD, CFM_ITALIC and
CFM_UNDERLINE named and zero, and EM_SETTARGETDEVICE(0, 0).

That last clause is why these were re-taken. The first set was made before
`rp_create` named the effects, and riched20's default face is System, which
is bold -- so every character came back `B--` from the machine against `---`
from ours, in every scenario, on documents where nothing had been bolded.
Seven findings that were one sentence: the two sides did not start from the
same character format.

**A dump is an answer to the question a particular `rp_create` asked, and
nothing inside the file records which.** The sha above is the mitigation and
not the fix; the fix is a line in the dump itself, which is dump.h's to emit
so that both sides emit it identically.

Two things a reader should know before trusting a diff against these.

`char` and `para` cover one index past the last visible character, because
riched20's document always ends in a paragraph mark: `type a` gives len 2.
That is the machine's model and ours does not have it yet.

The three sequences written to wrap now do. ES_AUTOHSCROLL in the style word
turns word wrap off, and EM_SETTARGETDEVICE(0, 0) turns it back on over that
bit -- measured in wrapon.txt -- which is what WordPad's own wrap-to-window
does. rp_create sends it, so `line` means something in 03, 06 and 07:

    03-indent-then-type        line 1 at 69
    06-resize-while-selected   line 1 at 40
    07-type-across-a-wrap      line 1 at 72

All nine were re-taken when rp_create changed, not only the three expected
to move, and the other six came back byte-identical. That check is the
point: a dump is an answer to the question a particular rp_create asked, so
"only these three can have changed" is a prediction and not a fact until it
is compared.


Re-taken at db32b52, which records `client W H` in the dump and sizes the
control the way the call asks. Both matter: the earlier set was taken in a
control 408 wide, because 0x550081C4 contains WS_MAXIMIZE and a child
created with it fills its parent's client area -- see maxq.txt. Ours honours
the requested size, so the two sides were laying text out in different
widths and every wrap comparison between them was meaningless.

With the widths equal and printed, three sequences that wrap say something
for the first time:

  07   ours 43, 33          machine 43, 33        identical
  03   ours 2 lines 44,39   machine 3 lines 35,43,5
  06   ours 44, 24          machine 40, 28        both at client 256

07 agreeing is the useful half: plain wrapping is right, and what looked
like a difference was the control size. 03 and 06 are real, at identical
widths, with both sides printing the width they used.
